WEBVTT

00:00:03.088 --> 00:00:08.138
Let’s look at three moments that often happen in this conversation, and what you can do.

00:00:08.188 --> 00:00:12.418
Moment one: “Our purpose is just what our boss tells us.”

00:00:12.468 --> 00:00:17.298
Some teams feel their purpose is given to them from above. That’s partly true.

00:00:17.348 --> 00:00:24.238
But even within a given mandate, there’s a lot of room for how the team understands it, and who they really serve.

00:00:24.288 --> 00:00:27.258
Say: “You’re right that some of it is given.

00:00:27.308 --> 00:00:30.418
Within that, what do we want to be known for?”

00:00:30.468 --> 00:00:33.378
Moment two: endless wordsmithing.

00:00:33.428 --> 00:00:39.778
The group spends twenty minutes on whether it should be “support” or “enable”. Stop it kindly.

00:00:39.828 --> 00:00:41.738
Say: “We have the meaning.

00:00:41.788 --> 00:00:43.678
The words can be polished later.”

00:00:43.728 --> 00:00:46.658
Then use the two-volunteer trick, and move on.

00:00:46.708 --> 00:00:51.458
A good-enough statement the team owns beats a perfect one nobody remembers.

00:00:51.508 --> 00:00:55.598
Moment three: a real disagreement about what the team is for.

00:00:55.648 --> 00:01:01.858
For example, half the team thinks you serve internal stakeholders, the other half thinks you serve end customers.

00:01:01.908 --> 00:01:04.398
Don’t smooth this over. It’s gold.

00:01:04.448 --> 00:01:07.898
It’s probably the reason for friction you’ve been feeling for months.

00:01:07.948 --> 00:01:13.518
Write both versions down, and ask: “What would be different in our work if it were one or the other?”

00:01:13.568 --> 00:01:18.638
If it can’t be settled in the room, it may need a decision from you, or from your own manager.

00:01:18.688 --> 00:01:21.038
And finally: test the result.

00:01:21.088 --> 00:01:24.958
Read the statement out loud and ask: “Would we say this to a friend?

00:01:25.008 --> 00:01:27.078
Does it help us say no to something?”

00:01:27.128 --> 00:01:30.603
If both answers are yes, write it on the canvas. You’re done for now.

